      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nCertificate details:\n- OIML Certificate No.: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2021.05\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Person title (page 2): RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body — hmm, the person responsible is Jan Kalandra on page 1, but the signature is by Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. The schema says issuing_authority has person_responsible, person_title. I'll use Jan Kalandra as person_responsible. Actually the \"Head of Certification Body\" title goes with Pavel Klenovský who signed. Hmm. Let me keep person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and person_title could be null or \"Head of Certification Body\"? The title belongs to Klenovský, not Kalandra. To avoid misattribution, I might omit person_title or set it to null. Actually, I could note the signer separately but schema doesn't have that field. I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and omit person_title since it belongs to a different person. Actually, maybe include it anyway... no, rule 8 says don't fabricate. The title is associated with Klenovský's signature. I'll leave person_title out.\n\n- Date issued: 15 October 2021 (Date: 15 October 2021 on page 2)\n- OIML issuer ID: not present explicitly. The logo \"V 3112\" might be a form number. CMI's OIML issuer ID... not stated. Omit.\n\n- Applicant: APATOR POWOGAZ S.A., ul. Klemensa Janickiego 23/25; 60-542 Poznań; Poland\n- Manufacturer: same\n\n- Certified type: water meter - ultrasonic, ULTRIMIS (UL)\n- Category: \"Water meter\" — the schema gives category examples. It's a water meter (ultrasonic). Category: \"Water meter\".\n- Type designations: ULTRIMIS (UL); models: UL 2.5, UL 4, UL 6.3, UL 10, UL 16, UL25 (UL 25)\n- Module designation: \"-\" → Not applicable (the field is \"if applicable\" and contains \"-\", so \"Not applicable\" or null). I'll use null since it's \"-\". Actually rule says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. The \"-\" means not applicable, so \"Not applicable\".\n\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy class 2\n- Scheme A\n\nTest reports:\n1. No. 0511-ER-V029-21 dated 7 October 2021, 62 pages including annex 1 — role: type_evaluation_report\n2. Test report No. 6015-PT-P5006-21, 91 pages including annex 1, 2, 3 — role: test_report\n3. Test report No. 6015-PT-P5007-21, 53 pages including annex 1 — role: test_report\n4. Software validation test report No. 6011-PT-SW024-21, 3 pages — role: test_report\n5. EMC test report No. 8551-PT-E0054-21, 7 pages including annex 1 — role: test_report\n\nDocumentation file: 0511-UL-V029-21 — documentation_file\n\nRevision history:\n- Addition 0, 15 October 2021, Issuing certificate\n\nPage total: 6\n\nNow the characteristics. This is a family of 6 models: UL 2.5, UL 4, UL 6.3, UL 10, UL 16, UL 25.\n\nNominal diameter varies per model but note UL 2.5 has two nominal diameters (15 and 20 as separate columns). The main table:\n\n- Model number: UL 2.5 (spans 2 columns: DN 15, DN 20), UL 4 (DN 20), UL 6.3 (DN 25), UL 10 (DN 25/32), UL 16 (DN 40), UL25 (DN 50)\n\nSo the model variants are actually per DN within model number? UL 2.5 has two nominal diameters. The flowrates table shows \"UL 2.5, DN 15/20\" with 3 columns each (Q3/Q1 = 800, 400, 250). So the actual variants are model × DN × ratio.\n\nHmm. How to model this? The schema allows model_level with values keyed by model. I could define models as \"UL 2.5 DN15\", \"UL 2.5 DN20\", \"UL 4\", \"UL 6.3\", \"UL 10\", \"UL 16\", \"UL 25\". That gives 7 models.\n\nThe ratio variants (Q3/Q1 = 800/400/250) are configuration axes essentially. The flowrate tables give Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 for each ratio.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest per the schema:\n\n- model_family.models: 7 entries: UL 2.5 (DN 15), UL 2.5 (DN 20), UL 4 (DN 20), UL 6.3 (DN 25), UL 10 (DN 25/32), UL 16 (DN 40), UL 25 (DN 50)\n\n- matrix_tables: preserve the flowrates tables and the main characteristics table.\n\n- characteristics.type_level: shared attributes: q2_over_q1 = 1.6, q3_over_q4 = 1.25, measuring_principle = ultrasonic, accuracy_class = 2, mpe_lower_zone = ±5%, mpe_upper_zone (two conditions: ≤30°C → ±2%, >30°C → ±3%) — that's config-level actually. Temperature class: T30, T50 (both allowed), water pressure class MAP10, MAP16, environmental class B or O, electromagnetic environment E1, E2, max admissible temperature 50°C, max admissible pressure 1.6 MPa, orientation any, indicating range 999999 m³, resolution 0.001 m³, resolution rapid testing 0.00001 m³, Umax 3.6 V, Umin 1.9 V, battery life 10 years, minimum straight inlet 0 mm, outlet 0 mm, installation sensitivity U0D0.\n\n- characteristics.model_level: nominal_diameter, q3_over_q1 (varies: UL2.5&UL4&UL6.3&UL10: 800/400/250; UL16: 400/250/800? wait let me re-check), pressure_loss_class (ΔP40 for all but UL25 which is ΔP25), connection_type, length brass, length composite.\n\nWait, UL 16 table is weird: Q1 row: 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.040, 0.064 — 5 columns for ratios 400, 250, 800, 400, 250. And Q3: 10.00, 10.00, 16.00, 16.00, 16.00. So UL 16 has Q3=10 with ratios 400 and 250, and Q3=16 with ratios 800, 400, 250. Interesting — so UL 16 actually has two sub-variants (Q3=10 and Q3=16). Hmm, that complicates. The first characteristics table says UL 16 spans DN 40 and ratios 800; 400; 250. The flowrate table for UL 16 shows 5 columns: (Q3=10, R400), (Q3=10, R250), (Q3=16, R800), (Q3=16, R400), (Q3=16, R250).\n\nGiven complexity, maybe simplest to treat flowrates via matrix_tables preserving the tables, and put a reasonable model_level extraction.\n\nLet me decide: models list from the main characteristics table: UL 2.5, UL 4, UL 6.3, UL 10, UL 16, UL 25. But nominal diameter for UL 2.5 is \"15 / 20\" and UL 10 \"25 / 32\".\n\nApproach: Use models keyed as \"UL 2.5\" etc., and for attributes that vary within a model (like nominal diameter 15/20), list both values or use sub-model IDs.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest faithful approach given the flowrate tables treats each column as a variant: model_id = \"UL 2.5 R800\" etc. But that's 3 variants per DN for UL2.5 (two DNs each with 3 ratios = 6 columns... wait the UL 2.5 flowrate table shows DN \"15 / 20\" with 3 columns, meaning both DN share the same flowrates per ratio).\n\nHmm, the UL2.5 table: Nominal diameter (DN): 15 / 20 (spans 3 columns). Q1: 0.003, 0.006, 0.010 for ratios 800, 400, 250. So UL 2.5 in DN15 or DN20 with ratio R800 → Q1=0.003.\n\nFor model_level, I could define models as model_id like \"UL 2.5\", with model_level entries having values per model, but where ratio matters, use config_level with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\".\n\nLet me structure:\n\nmodels: UL 2.5, UL 4, UL 6.3, UL 10, UL 16, UL 25 (6 models).\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- nominal_diameter: UL 2.5: \"15 / 20\" (or 15 and 20 — hmm). Values: could be value as string \"15 / 20\"? The rule says numbers not strings, but this is genuinely two values. Could represent as {min:15, max:20}? That's wrong semantically. I'll use string \"15 / 20\" or list. Actually value can be string. I'll use \"15/20\" hmm. Alternatively define models as DN-specific: \"UL 2.5 DN15\", \"UL 2.5 DN20\", etc. That makes nominal_diameter numeric per model. And then ratios handled via config axis or via matrix table.\n\nI think DN-specific model IDs are cleaner:\n- UL 2.5 DN15\n- UL 2.5 DN20\n- UL 4 DN20\n- UL 6.3 DN25\n- UL 10 DN25\n- UL 10 DN32\n- UL 16 DN40\n- UL 25 DN50\n\nHmm but UL 10 \"25/32\" means connection sizes maybe. DN 25/32 - probably DN25 body with G1¼B or G1½B threads. It's ambiguous.\n\nLet me not over-engineer. I'll keep 6 models (UL 2.5, UL 4, UL 6.3, UL 10, UL 16, UL 25) and:\n\n- nominal_diameter as model_level with values: \"15\", \"20\" for UL2.5... The value could be a list [15, 20]. Schema says value is number | {min,max} | string. A list isn't allowed. I could do two separate value entries? The values[] array is per model — one value per model presumably. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: string \"15 / 20\" for UL 2.5 and \"25 / 32\" for UL 10. That's acceptable — value as string since it's not a single number.\n\n- q3_over_q1: model_level: UL 2.5: \"800; 400; 250\" — but the flowrates table gives per-ratio Q values. These ratios are configuration variants. Rule 5: config_level with axis. So:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: q1, unit m³/h, axis: model × q3_q1_ratio. Values with condition like \"UL 2.5 DN15/20, Q3/Q1=800\", value 0.003.\n\nHmm, that's a lot of entries but it's the faithful way. Actually matrix_tables is designed to \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — the flowrate tables fit matrix_tables nicely. Then model_level for the simpler varying attributes.\n\nLet me do both: matrix_tables for the four flowrate tables (fully preserved), and model_level for main varying attributes (nominal_diameter, q3_over_q1 options, pressure_loss_class, connection_type, length_brass_body, length_composite_body).\n\nLet me extract per-model varying data:\n\nMain table:\n| attribute | UL 2.5 | UL 4 | UL 6.3 | UL 10 | UL 16 | UL 25 |\n- nominal_diameter: 15/20, 20, 25, 25/32, 40, 50\n- q3_over_q1: \"800; 400; 250\", same, same, same, \"800; 400; 250\" (colspan 6 covers first 6 columns = UL2.5..UL16), \"500; 400; 250\"\n  Wait: the row has colspan=6 \"800; 400; 250\" then UL25 \"500; 400; 250\". The 8 columns: label + 7 data columns (UL2.5 spans 2). colspan 6 covers UL2.5(2)+UL4+UL6.3+UL10+UL16 = 6. Yes. So UL16 also 800;400;250 (though flowrate table shows Q3=10 only at R400/R250 and Q3=16 at R800/400/250 — combined it's 800;400;250 with Q3 varying).\n- pressure_loss_class: ΔP40 (colspan 6: UL2.5–UL16), ΔP25 (UL25)\n- connection_type: UL 2.5 (2 cols): \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\" — hmm the cell content: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\" spans 2 columns. So UL 2.5 DN15: G ¾ B or G ⅞ B; UL 2.5 DN20: G ¾ B or G 1 B. OCR mangled. I'll record as string: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B (DN15); G ¾ B or G 1 B (DN20)\"? That's fabricating a bit. Let me keep raw: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\". Hmm.\n\nActually for the model_level nominal diameter, since UL2.5 spans two DNs, maybe models should be DN-level after all. Let me reconsider: 7 variants:\n1. UL 2.5, DN15: G¾B or G⅞B, brass length 80-165, composite 80-110\n2. UL 2.5, DN20: G¾B or G1B, brass 80-165, composite 80-110\n3. UL 4, DN20: G1B, brass 105-190, composite 105-130\n4. UL 6.3, DN25: G1¼B, brass 105-190, composite 105-130\n5. UL 10, DN25/32: G1¼B or G1½B, brass 165-260, composite: \"-\" \n6. UL 16, DN40: G2B or flange, brass 260 (or 300?) \n7. UL 25, DN50: G2½B or flange, brass 200-300, composite: -\n\nWait, the length brass row: \"Length [mm] – brass body\": cells: \"80 –165\" (colspan2), \"105-190\", \"105 -190\", \"165 -260\", \"260\", \"300\", \"200 - 300\". That's 7 data cells for 7 columns... but the label row structure: first data col span 2 (UL2.5), then UL4, UL6.3, UL10, UL16, UL25 = 7 columns total. But we have 7 cells: 80-165, 105-190, 105-190, 165-260, 260, 300, 200-300. That's one extra! \n\nHmm: 80-165 (UL2.5 DN15+DN20), 105-190 (UL4), 105-190 (UL6.3), 165-260 (UL10), 260 (UL16?), 300 (?), 200-300 (UL25). The last row of the table might be misaligned by OCR. Possibly UL16 = 260-300? Or UL16=260, UL25=300... and 200-300 extra? Actually UL25 DN50 standard length is 200-300? Hmm, but there's an extra cell.\n\nCompare with connection row: cells: colspan2 (\"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\"), \"G 1 B\", \"G 1 ¼ B\", \"G 1 ¼ B or G 1 ½ B\", \"G 2 B or flange\", \"G 2 ½ B or flange\" = 6 data cells for 7 columns (first spans 2). Good.\n\nLength brass row has 7 data cells where first spans 2 → 8 columns worth? That means one cell too many — likely OCR split \"260-300\" into \"260\" and \"300\" for UL16, and then \"200 - 300\" for UL25. Or UL16 brass = \"260\" hmm. DN40 water meters typically have length 260 mm (L=260) or 300. UL25 DN50: 200-300? Actually DN50 lengths: 200, 250, 300. Plausible: UL16: 260 or 300; UL25: 200-300.\n\nHmm wait — count again the table columns. Header row: \"Model number:\" + colspan2 \"UL 2.5\" + \"UL 4\" + \"UL 6.3\" + \"UL 10\" + \"UL 16\" + \"UL25\" = 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 8 columns. So 7 data columns.\n\nNominal diameter row: 15, 20, 20, 25, 25/32, 40, 50 = 7 cells. Good.\n\nBrass length row: 80–165 (span2), 105-190, 105-190, 165-260, 260, 300, 200-300 = span2 + 6 = 8 columns? span2 + 6 singles = 7 data columns... wait: colspan2 counts as 2, then 105-190, 105-190, 165-260, 260, 300, 200-300 = 6 more → total 8 data columns, but only 7 exist. So one too many. Given \"Length 80–165\" for UL2.5 spans the 2 DN columns, remaining columns UL4, UL6.3, UL10, UL16, UL25 = 5 columns, but 6 cells remain. Likely \"260\" and \"300\" belong together as \"260-300\" for UL16, and \"200 - 300\" for UL25. Hmm, but alternatively UL16 = 260, UL25 = 300... no wait then \"200-300\" would be for a column that doesn't exist.\n\nActually wait, maybe the composite row: 80-110 (span2), 105-130, 105-130, -, -, -, - = span2 + 6 singles = 8 columns again. Hmm, also one too many: UL4=105-130, UL6.3=105-130, then UL10, UL16, UL25 = \"-\" ×3, but there are 4 \"-\" cells. So both rows have one extra cell. So probably the colspan2 for UL2.5 should be colspan... hmm, or the first cell spans 2 and OCR added an extra. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rows are meant as: UL2.5: 80-165; UL4: 105-190; UL6.3: 105-190; UL10: 165-260; UL16: 260; UL25: 300... and \"200-300\" extra? That doesn't work either since UL25 shown as \"200 - 300\" at the end.\n\nLet me count composite row cells: \"80 -110\" (colspan 2), \"105-130\", \"105 -130\", \"-\", \"-\", \"-\", \"-\" → 2+1+1+1+1+1+1 = 8. With 7 data columns: UL2.5(2)=80-110, UL4=105-130, UL6.3=105-130, UL10=-, UL16=-, UL25=-. That leaves one extra \"-\". OCR error likely added an extra cell or the colspan should be 2 for first and the dash count is 3. Since dashes are indistinguishable, fine.\n\nFor brass: UL2.5=80-165, UL4=105-190, UL6.3=105-190, UL10=165-260, UL16=260 (or 260-300), UL25=200-300. Given ambiguity, I'd say UL16 brass length = \"260\" and... hmm. DN40 meter lengths: standard 260 mm. Some are 300. \"260, 300\"? \n\nGiven \"260\" and \"300\" as separate cells followed by \"200 - 300\", the most sensible: UL16: 260–300 (brass lengths 260 or 300), UL25: 200–300. Or UL16: 260, UL25: 300, extra \"200-300\" spurious. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe I'm miscounting because \"Length\" row might not have the first cell spanning 2 in the original; maybe UL 2.5 DN15 = 80-165? No wait, the main table earlier: first data col spans 2 columns (UL 2.5 has 2 sub-columns for DN15/DN20).\n\nLet me just go: UL16 = \"260\" and note in matrix table preserving raw. Actually, I'll preserve the raw table in matrix_tables with the cells as-is (7 cells including the extra), and in model_level use:\n- length_brass_body: UL 2.5: {min:80,max:165}, UL 4: {min:105,max:190}, UL 6.3: {min:105,max:190}, UL 10: {min:165,max:260}, UL 16: \"260\" hmm...\n\nI'll go with UL 16: {min:260, max:300} — interpreting \"260\"/\"300\" split as \"260-300\"? Risky. Alternatively value \"260\" and treat \"300\" as separate... \n\nHmm, let me think about real Apator Powogaz ULTRIMIS. DN40 body lengths: 260 mm is standard for DN40 (L=260). DN50: 200-300 (L=200 or 300). Actually DN50 water meter lengths are commonly 200 mm? Hmm, DN50 meters: 165-600? Common lengths for DN50: 200, 250, 280, 300. So UL25: 200-300 makes sense. And DN40: 260 or 300? DN40 lengths: 260 typical; also 300 exists. So UL16 = \"260 or 300\"? Hmm.\n\nI'll record UL16 brass length as {min: 260, max: 300} with a note... no notes allowed per-value except footnotes. Alternatively preserve exactly: UL16: \"260\" and UL25: \"300\"... then \"200-300\" unassigned. No.\n\nDecision: matrix_tables will preserve the raw installation details table exactly as OCR'd. For model_level, I'll use: UL 16: {min:260, max:300}, UL 25: {min:200, max:300}. This is the most sensible reading (the \"260\"/\"300\" pair split across two cells due to line-break OCR: \"260\" then \"300\" — hmm, actually the cells are \"260\" and \"300\" separately then \"200 - 300\").\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: `<td align=\"center\">260</td><td align=\"center\">300</td><td align=\"center\">200 - 300</td>`. Could be OCR split \"260\\n300\" hmm. Or UL16=260, and... you know what, there might actually be a hidden colspan issue where the UL16 column spans 2 because UL16 could have... no.\n\nI'll go with UL 16: \"260\" (single) — no wait. Ugh.\n\nLet me count UL16 vs extra: If UL16=260 and UL25=300, then \"200-300\" has no column → impossible, OCR wouldn't invent content. If UL16=260-300 (OCR split \"260-\" and \"300\"... the hyphen could have been lost), UL25=200-300 → all content used, column count matches if UL16 spans... no, column count is off by one either way.\n\nActually! Maybe the first cell \"80 –165\" spans only... The row shows colspan=\"2\" explicitly in OCR. But maybe in the original PDF, the brass length row has cells: UL2.5 DN15: 80-165, UL2.5 DN20: 80-165 (merged), UL4: 105-190, UL6.3: 105-190, UL10: 165-260, UL16: 260, UL25: 300, and... no, still 200-300 extra.\n\nOR: UL 25 column in the original table might span 2 physical columns in some rows? The pressure loss row: colspan6 ΔP40 + ΔP25 = 7. OK.\n\nOR: the composite row and brass row might have 7 single cells (no colspan): 80-165 / 105-190 ... meaning DN15: 80-165, DN20: 105-190?? Unlikely physically (DN15 and DN20 lengths both 80-165... typical lengths: DN15: 80,110,165; DN20: 110,130,190,165...). Hmm! Actually that's plausible too: DN15 lengths 80-165, DN20 105-190? And DN20(UL4) 105-190, DN25 105-190... wait no.\n\nComposite row: 80-110, 105-130, 105-130, -, -, -, -: if no merge: DN15: 80-110, DN20: 105-130, DN20(UL4): 105-130, DN25: -, 25/32: -, 40: -, 50: -. That works with 7 columns, NO colspan! Brass: DN15: 80-165, DN20: 105-190, DN20(UL4): 105-190, DN25: 105-190?? no wait: cells: 80–165, 105-190, 105 -190, 165 -260, 260, 300, 200 - 300: DN15: 80-165, DN20: 105-190, UL4 DN20: 105-190, UL6.3 DN25: 165-260?? Hmm DN25 length 165-260: DN25 lengths: 110? 130? 165? 190? 260? plausible 165-260. UL10 DN25/32: 260, ... then UL16: 300, UL25: 200-300. That works with 7 columns and NO colspan merge!\n\nBut the OCR explicitly says colspan=\"2\" for the first cell. If that's accurate, then there are 8 cells worth. If OCR is wrong about colspan, the no-merge reading fits perfectly: \n- brass: UL2.5/DN15: 80–165, UL2.5/DN20: 105–190, UL4: 105–190, UL6.3: 165–260, UL10: 260, UL16: 300, UL25: 200–300.\n\nHmm, DN25 at 165-260 vs DN25/32 at 260? and DN40 at 300? DN40=300 only? Hmm, DN40 lengths: 260 or 300. DN25: 165-260. DN25/32: 260? That seems odd.\n\nVersus merge reading: UL2.5: 80-165, UL4: 105-190, UL6.3: 105-190, UL10: 165-260, UL16: 260 or 300 (split cells), UL25: 200-300. Also plausible.\n\nI can't resolve definitively. I'll preserve raw in matrix_tables (with the cells as given) and for model_level use the merge reading with UL16 = \"260; 300\" hmm — value as string \"260 / 300\"? Or since it's ambiguous...\n\nActually, let me reconsider: which is more physically sensible for ultrasonic meters? Apator Powogaz ULTRIMIS-W (water): DN15/20 lengths: 80-190? I recall ultrimis has lengths 80, 110, 130, 165, 190, 260, 300, 350... For a DN40 ultrasonic meter, 260 mm and 300 mm are both common. For DN50: 200, 250, 300.\n\nMerge reading gives UL16: 260 & 300 (two cells) → lengths 260 or 300. UL25: 200-300. This is very sensible! DN40: 260 or 300; DN50: 200-300.\n\nNo-merge reading gives DN20: 105-190 (but composite DN20: 105-130 vs UL4 DN20 composite 105-130 — same), UL6.3 DN25: 165-260 (but composite UL6.3: 105-130 — inconsistent with brass 165-260? possible), UL10: 260, UL16: 300 only.\n\nHmm. The colspan=2 is explicit in the OCR HTML. The extra cell is the anomaly. OCR could have hallucinated colspan. \n\nGiven the UL 2.5 model has TWO nominal diameters and the nominal diameter row has separate cells for 15 and 20, while connection type cell spans 2, the table merges per attribute. For lengths, it's likely also merged (both DN share lengths).\n\nFinal decision: merge reading. model_level length_brass_body: UL2.5: 80-165, UL4: 105-190, UL6.3: 105-190, UL10: 165-260, UL16: \"260; 300\" → I'll represent as string \"260 or 300\"? Rules: value number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string \"260 / 300\". Hmm, or {min:260, max:300} — that misrepresents (implies range) but... \"260 or 300\" as string is more honest. I'll use string \"260 or 300\". Hmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider once more — maybe UL16 brass is genuinely \"260–300\" as a range (i.e., lengths 260 and 300 available). Range {min:260, max:300} conveys available lengths. Eh. I'll go with string \"260 or 300\" for honesty? The matrix table preserves raw anyway. Actually for consistency with others which are ranges like 80-165 (meaning available lengths 80 to 165 in steps), 260-300 could equally be a range. I'll use {min:260, max:300}. Hmm, but then why two cells \"260\" \"300\"... \n\nOK executive decision: UL 16 brass: {min: 260, max: 300}; UL 25 brass: {min: 200, max: 300}. Matrix table preserves raw. Moving on.\n\nComposite: UL2.5: 80-110, UL4: 105-130, UL6.3: 105-130, UL10: null (\"-\"), UL16: null, UL25: null. The \"-\" means not available. I'll just omit values or set value null. I'll include with value null? Rule: empty strings not values, use null or omit. I'll omit composite for UL10/16/25 — or include with null to show unavailability. I'll include with value null to convey \"not available\". Hmm, actually cleaner: only include models where data exists, and note in matrix table. I'll include null values — the schema example shows value can be null? \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" So null allowed. I'll use null.\n\nConnection type (screw thread): UL2.5: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\" (spans both DNs: DN15: G¾B or G⅞B; DN20: G¾B or G1B). Since I'm using model IDs \"UL 2.5\" (not DN-split), I'll put the combined string: \"G 3/4 B or G 7/8 B (DN 15); G 3/4 B or G 1 B (DN 20)\" — that's interpreting. Raw: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\". Hmm. Given the cell shows \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\", and DN 15/20 exist, the natural reading is DN15 → G¾B or G⅞B, DN20 → G¾B or G1B. I'll split models into DN-specific after all?? \n\nYou know, let me just split UL 2.5 into DN15 and DN20 as separate model entries. Then:\n- UL 2.5 (DN 15): nominal_diameter 15, connection \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B\", brass 80-165, composite 80-110\n- UL 2.5 (DN 20): nominal_diameter 20, connection \"G ¾ B or G 1 B\", brass 80-165, composite 80-110\n\nAnd UL 10 keeps DN \"25 / 32\", connection \"G 1 ¼ B or G 1 ½ B\". Hmm, DN 25/32 — that's likely DN25 nominal with G1¼B or DN32? Connection G1¼B or G1½B suggests DN25 or DN32 threads. So UL 10 could also be DN-split: 25 and 32. But lengths are same (165-260). I'll keep UL 10 as one model with nominal_diameter \"25 / 32\" string.\n\nHmm, but then flowrate tables: \"UL 2.5 DN 15/20\" shares flowrates across both DNs — fine, model_level q values would be per ratio config anyway.\n\nModel IDs: I'll use: \"UL 2.5 DN15\", \"UL 2.5 DN20\", \"UL 4 DN20\", \"UL 6.3 DN25\", \"UL 10 DN25/32\", \"UL 16 DN40\", \"UL 25 DN50\". Hmm, or simpler \"UL 2.5 (DN15)\". I'll go with e.g. \"UL 2.5 DN15\".\n\nActually hold on — do I want to overcomplicate? The alternative: models = [\"UL 2.5\", \"UL 4\", \"UL 6.3\", \"UL 10\", \"UL 16\", \"UL 25\"] and nominal_diameter values: \"15 / 20\", 20, 25, \"25 / 32\", 40, 50. Connection for UL2.5: the raw combined string. That's simpler and less interpretive. The type_designations would list the 6 model numbers.\n\nI think I'll go with the 6 model numbers as model_id (matching \"Model number\" row), since the certificate itself organizes by model number. nominal_diameter for UL 2.5: value \"15 / 20\" (string) — it's a set {15, 20}. Eh, I could do value: \"15 / 20\". Hmm, rules prefer numbers... but this is genuinely two values. I'll use string.\n\nHmm, but the connection-type interpretation \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\" — I'll keep raw string. OK.\n\nWait, but then for UL 10: nominal diameter \"25 / 32\" and connection \"G 1 ¼ B or G 1 ½ B\". Fine.\n\nNow flowrates: config_level with axis \"model × q3_q1_ratio\":\n\nFor UL 2.5 (DN 15/20), ratios 800/400/250:\n- q1: 0.003 / 0.006 / 0.010\n- q2: 0.005 / 0.010 / 0.016\n- q3: 2.50 each\n- q4: 3.13 each\n\nUL 4 (DN20): q1: 0.005/0.010/0.016; q2: 0.008/0.016/0.026; q3: 4.00; q4: 5.00\nUL 6.3 (DN25): q1: 0.008/0.016/0.025; q2: 0.013/0.025/0.040; q3: 6.30; q4: 7.88\nUL 10 (DN25/32): q1: 0.013/0.025/0.040; q2: 0.020/0.040/0.064; q3: 10.00; q4: 12.50\nUL 16 (DN40): 5 columns: (Q3=10, R400): q1 0.025, q2 0.040, q4 12.50; (Q3=10, R250): q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 12.50; (Q3=16, R800): q1 0.020, q2 0.032, q4 20.00; (Q3=16, R400): q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 20.00; (Q3=16, R250): q1 0.064, q2 0.102, q4 20.00.\n\nWait the UL16 table Q1 row: 0.025, 0.040, 0.020, 0.040, 0.064. Q3/Q1 row: 400, 250, 800, 400, 250. Q3: 10, 10, 16, 16, 16. So:\n- (Q3=10, R400): q1=0.025, q2=0.040, q4=12.50\n- (Q3=10, R250): q1=0.040, q2=0.064, q4=12.50\n- (Q3=16, R800): q1=0.020, q2=0.032, q4=20.00\n- (Q3=16, R400): q1=0.040, q2=0.064, q4=20.00\n- (Q3=16, R250): q1=0.064, q2=0.102, q4=20.00\n\nUL 25 (DN50): R500: q1 0.050, q2 0.080, q3 25.0, q4 31.3; R400: q1 0.0625, q2 0.100; R250: q1 0.100, q2 0.160.\n\nconfig_level entries: attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, each with axis \"model_and_q3_q1_ratio\", values: condition strings like \"UL 2.5, Q3/Q1=800\".\n\nNumber of entries: q1: 6+3+3+3+5+3 = 23; ×4 attributes = 92 entries. That's a lot but it's the faithful structured way. Alternatively, matrix_tables preserve the tables and model_level gives q3_over_q1 options per model. The schema says matrix_tables preserve \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". The flowrate tables DO fit config_level... but 92 entries is huge. Hmm.\n\nThe instructions emphasize model_level for varying attributes and matrix_tables for preservation. The R137 example extracted flowrates BOTH into matrix_tables AND model_level. So I'll do: matrix_tables for the flowrate tables (4 tables) + config_level for q1..q4.\n\nHmm, wait — should q3 also be config_level? q3 is constant within model (2.50 for UL2.5 across ratios) but varies by model → model_level! Same for q4. So:\n- model_level: q3 (2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 16.00, 25.0), q4 (3.13, 5.00, 7.88, 12.50, 20.00/12.50??, 31.3).\n\nHmm UL16 q4 varies: 12.50 (Q3=10) and 20.00 (Q3=16). So q4 for UL16 depends on ratio config. Ugh. UL16 is the messy one.\n\nOK here's my final structure decision:\n\n1. models: 6 model numbers.\n2. model_level:\n   - nominal_diameter: UL2.5: \"15 / 20\" (string), UL4: 20, UL6.3: 25, UL10: \"25 / 32\", UL16: 40, UL25: 50\n   - q3 (m³/h): 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, \"10 or 16\"?? hmm UL16 has both 10 and 16.\n   \nUL16 really has two sub-variants. Options: make models \"UL 16 (Q3=10)\" and \"UL 16 (Q3=16)\". Then 7 models total:\n- UL 2.5 (DN 15/20)\n- UL 4 (DN 20)\n- UL 6.3 (DN 25)\n- UL 10 (DN 25/32)\n- UL 16 (Q3=10, DN 40)\n- UL 16 (Q3=16, DN 40)\n- UL 25 (DN 50)\n\nThat resolves UL16's messiness: q3: 10 vs 16; q4: 12.50 vs 20; ratios: 400/250 vs 800/400/250.\n\nI'll do that: model_ids: \"UL 2.5\", \"UL 4\", \"UL 6.3\", \"UL 10\", \"UL 16 (Q3=10)\", \"UL 16 (Q3=16)\", \"UL 25\". Hmm, or include DN in ID... The IDs are getting synthetic but that's what the R60 example does (\"Emax=25t\").\n\nLet me finalize models:\n1. \"UL 2.5 (DN 15)\"  — no wait, if I keep UL2.5 single with DN \"15 / 20\"...\n\nFinal final: 7 models:\n- UL 2.5: nominal_diameter \"15 / 20\"\n- UL 4: 20\n- UL 6.3: 25\n- UL 10: \"25 / 32\"\n- UL 16 (Q3=10): 40\n- UL 16 (Q3=16): 40\n- UL 25: 50\n\nmodel_level attributes:\n- nominal_diameter (mm? DN unitless): values as above; unit null (DN dimensionless designator).\n- q3 [m³/h]: 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00, 25.0\n- q4 [m³/h]: 3.13, 5.00, 7.88, 12.50, 12.50, 20.00, 31.3\n- q3_over_q1_options: \"800; 400; 250\" ×4, \"400; 250\", \"800; 400; 250\", \"500; 400; 250\" — these are the available ratios. Could be config axis instead. I'll make it model_level with string values or arrays... value as string \"800; 400; 250\". OK.\n- pressure_loss_class: ΔP40 for all except UL25: ΔP25.\n- connection_type_screw_thread: strings per model:\n  - UL 2.5: \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\" — hmm with DN15/DN20. I'll write \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B (DN 15) / G ¾ B or G 1 B (DN 20)\"? That's interpretation but reasonable and clearer. Risky per \"don't fabricate\"... it's a faithful reading. I'll keep the raw string \"G ¾ B or G ⅞ B / G ¾ B or G 1 B\".\n  \n  Hmm, ¾ and ⅞ are unicode fractions. Keep as-is.\n  - UL 4: \"G 1 B\"\n  - UL 6.3: \"G 1 ¼ B\"\n  - UL 10: \"G 1 ¼ B or G 1 ½ B\"\n  - UL 16: \"G 2 B or flange\"\n  - UL 25: \"G 2 ½ B or flange\"\n- length_brass_body [mm]: UL2.5: {80,165}, UL4: {105,190}, UL6.3: {105,190}, UL10: {165,260}, UL16(both): {260,300}, UL25: {200,300}\n- length_composite_body [mm]: UL2.5: {80,110}, UL4: {105,130}, UL6.3: {105,130}, others: null\n\ntype_level characteristics (shared):\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_zone: -5 to +5% → value {min:-5, max:5}, unit \"%\"? Or \"±5%\" as string. I'll do value: {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\". Canonical label: mpe_lower_zone / maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone. Let me name: \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\".\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: temperature-dependent → config_level:\n  - attribute: mpe_upper_flowrate_zone, axis: water_temperature, values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: {min:-2,max:2}, unit \"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: {min:-3,max:3}, unit \"%\"}]\n  Alternatively type_level with two entries... config_level fits.\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q3_over_q4: 1.25\n- temperature_class: \"T30, T50\" — list of options; value string \"T30 / T50\" or... I'll use \"T30, T50\". Hmm, as a class designator string.\n- water_pressure_class (MAP): \"MAP 10, MAP 16\"\n- environmental_class: \"B or O\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E1, E2\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50, °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: 999999, m³ — \"999 999\" → 999999\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.001, m³\n- resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing: 0.00001, m³\n- eut_category: \"Ultrasonic water meters\"\n- eut_case: \"B\"\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0, mm\n- min_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0, mm\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\" → false? I'll use \"No\".\n- power_supply_type: \"non-replaceable battery\"\n- umax: 3.6, V\n- umin: 1.9, V\n- minimum_battery_life: 10, years\n- software versions: legally_relevant_software_versions: \"03.00 / 5563; 04.01 / 235E; 04.10 / 62C7611F\" — string.\n\nAlso from page 3 description: radio module frequencies 433/434/868 MHz — optional equipment not part of cert. Could note in description. High resolution mode volume resolution 0.00001 m³ (matches rapid testing resolution). Radio frequencies: I'll include in description text only.\n\nMounting: \"-\", wiring \"-\", etc. — omit.\n\nMatrix tables to preserve:\n1. \"Basic technical data by model\" — the main characteristics table (name: \"Basic technical data of water meters types ULTRIMIS (UL)\"). Columns: model_number, nominal_diameter, q3_over_q1, q2_over_q1, q3_over_q4, measuring_principle, accuracy_class, mpe_lower, mpe_upper, temperature_class, water_pressure_class, pressure_loss_class, environmental_class, electromagnetic_environment, max_admissible_temperature, max_admissible_pressure, orientation, indicating_range, resolution, rapid_test_resolution, eut_category, eut_case. Rows per model (with UL2.5 spanning DN15/20). This is somewhat redundant with model_level/type_level. Should I include it? The schema says preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. Since I extracted into model_level, maybe skip the main table and only include flowrate tables. Hmm. But installation details table (connection, lengths) also extracted to model_level. \n\nI'll include the flowrate tables (4 of them) as matrix_tables since flowrates vs ratio vs model is the multi-dim part. And maybe one combined \"installation details\" table. To be safe and complete, I'll include:\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. flowrates UL 2.5 and UL 4 (table on page 5)\n2. flowrates UL 6.3 and UL 10 (page 6 top)\n3. flowrates UL 16 (page 6)\n4. flowrates UL 25 (page 6)\n5. Installation details by model (connection type + lengths) — could combine.\n\nActually, maybe merge flowrate tables 1&2 conceptually but they're separate tables in the doc. I'll keep them as separate matrix_tables entries matching the document.\n\nFor matrix_tables rows, format per the R137 example: rows as objects with column keys, numeric values.\n\nTable 1 (UL 2.5 & UL 4): columns: model_number, dn, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1. Rows: 6 rows:\n- UL 2.5, DN \"15 / 20\", 0.003, 0.005, 2.50, 3.13, 800\n- UL 2.5, \"15 / 20\", 0.006, 0.010, 2.50, 3.13, 400\n- UL 2.5, \"15 / 20\", 0.010, 0.016, 2.50, 3.13, 250\n- UL 4, 20, 0.005, 0.008, 4.00, 5.00, 800\n- UL 4, 20, 0.010, 0.016, 4.00, 5.00, 400\n- UL 4, 20, 0.016, 0.026, 4.00, 5.00, 250\n\nTable 2 (UL 6.3 & UL 10): 6 rows similar.\nTable 3 (UL 16): 5 rows.\nTable 4 (UL 25): 3 rows.\n\nInstallation details table: columns: model_number, nominal_diameter, connection_type, length_brass_mm, length_composite_mm. Rows 6 (or 7 with UL2.5 DN split). I'll do 6 rows with UL2.5 connection raw string.\n\nNow config_level for flowrates by ratio: should I also emit config_level entries for q1, q2? Given matrix_tables + model_level q3/q4 cover it, and q1/q2 vary by ratio (config)... The instructions for R137 emitted model_level for qmax/qt/qmin. Here the varying axis