OWOGAZ S.A.\", address_lines: [\"ul. Klemensa Janickiego 23/25\", \"60-542 Poznań\", \"Poland\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — description says \"water meter - ultrasonic\". Category: \"Water meter\". \n- type_designations: [\"ULTRIMIS (UL)\", \"UL 2.5\", \"UL 4\", \"UL 6.3\", \"UL 10\", \"UL 16\", \"UL 25\"] — hmm, type designations should be type identifiers: \"ULTRIMIS (UL)\". Models are separate. The R60 example put models in type_designations? \"type_designations[]: all type/model identifiers mentioned\". So include all: [\"ULTRIMIS (UL)\", \"UL 2.5\", \"UL 4\", \"UL 6.3\", \"UL 10\", \"UL 16\", \"UL25\"]. I'll normalize \"UL25\" → \"UL 25\"? The doc writes \"UL25\" in the table header. I'll list \"UL 25\" (normalizing whitespace) — fine.\n- module_designation: \"Not applicable\" (marked \"-\")\n- description: from page 3: \"The ultrasonic water meters type ULTRIMIS (UL) are designed to measure, memorise and display the volume at metering conditions of water passing through the measurement transducer. ...\" I'll write a condensed description.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"ULTRIMIS (UL)\"\n- models: 7 entries as decided. Attributes {} per model? Could put nominal_diameter in attributes{}. Schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I can put nominal_diameter in attributes. But then also in model_level — redundant. I'll keep models simple with model_id and label, attributes minimal (maybe nominal_diameter). I'll include attributes: {nominal_diameter: ...}? Hmm, attributes{} shape isn't strictly defined (structured value?). I'll leave attributes out or empty to avoid schema conflicts, and put everything in characteristics.model_level. Actually let me add label: e.g. label \"ULTRIMIS UL 2.5, DN 15/20\". Fine.\n\nWait — should UL 2.5 be split DN15/DN20 in models? I decided to keep one \"UL 2.5\" with DN \"15 / 20\". And UL 10 one with \"25 / 32\". And UL16 split by Q3. Model IDs:\n1. \"UL 2.5\"\n2. \"UL 4\"\n3. \"UL 6.3\"\n4. \"UL 10\"\n5. \"UL 16 (Q3=10)\"\n6. \"UL 16 (Q3=16)\"\n7. \"UL 25\"\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. nominal_diameter: UL 2.5: \"15 / 20\"; UL 4: 20; UL 6.3: 25; UL 10: \"25 / 32\"; UL16s: 40; UL 25: 50. Unit: null (DN). \n2. q3 [m³/h]: 2.50, 4.00, 6.30, 10.00, 10.00, 16.00, 25.0\n3. q4 [m³/h]: 3.13, 5.00, 7.88, 12.50, 12.50, 20.00, 31.3\n4. q3_over_q1: \"800; 400; 250\", \"800; 400; 250\", \"800; 400; 250\", \"800; 400; 250\", \"400; 250\", \"800; 400; 250\", \"500; 400; 250\"\n5. pressure_loss_class: \"ΔP40\" ×6, \"ΔP25\"\n6. connection_type: strings\n7. length_brass_body: mm ranges\n8. length_composite_body: mm ranges / null\n\nconfig_level:\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone (axis: water_temperature): ≤30°C → ±2%, >30°C → ±3%\n- q1 (axis: model_and_q3_q1_ratio): 23 entries\n- q2 (axis: model_and_q3_q1_ratio): 23 entries\n\nHmm, q1/q2 config: 46 entries. Let me write them:\n\nUL 2.5 (DN 15/20):\n- R800: q1 0.003, q2 0.005\n- R400: q1 0.006, q2 0.010\n- R250: q1 0.010, q2 0.016\nUL 4 (DN 20):\n- R800: q1 0.005, q2 0.008\n- R400: q1 0.010, q2 0.016\n- R250: q1 0.016, q2 0.026\nUL 6.3 (DN 25):\n- R800: q1 0.008, q2 0.013\n- R400: q1 0.016, q2 0.025\n- R250: q1 0.025, q2 0.040\nUL 10 (DN 25/32):\n- R800: q1 0.013, q2 0.020\n- R400: q1 0.025, q2 0.040\n- R250: q1 0.040, q2 0.064\nUL 16 (Q3=10):\n- R400: q1 0.025, q2 0.040\n- R250: q1 0.040, q2 0.064\nUL 16 (Q3=16):\n- R800: q1 0.020, q2 0.032\n- R400: q1 0.040, q2 0.064\n- R250: q1 0.064, q2 0.102\nUL 25 (DN 50):\n- R500: q1 0.050, q2 0.080\n- R400: q1 0.0625, q2 0.100\n- R250: q1 0.100, q2 0.160\n\nThat's 20 conditions total (6+3+3+3+2+3+3 = 23? count: UL2.5: 3, UL4: 3, UL6.3: 3, UL10: 3, UL16(10): 2, UL16(16): 3, UL25: 3 = 20). Yes 20 conditions × 2 attributes = 40 entries.\n\nCondition format: \"UL 2.5 (DN 15/20), Q3/Q1 = 800\" — condition strings.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null/omit\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [2]\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"0511-ER-V029-21\", date: \"2021-10-07\", pages: 62, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n2. {id: \"6015-PT-P5006-21\", pages: 91, role: \"test_report\"}\n3. {id: \"6015-PT-P5007-21\", pages: 53, role: \"test_report\"}\n4. {id: \"6011-PT-SW024-21\", pages: 3, role: \"test_report\"} — software validation test report; role test_report.\n5. {id: \"8551-PT-E0054-21\", pages: 7, role: \"test_report\"}\n\ndocumentation file: 0511-UL-V029-21 → test_reports entry with role documentation_file? The schema has test_reports[].role including documentation_file. So: {id: \"0511-UL-V029-21\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"Addition 0\", date: \"2021-10-15\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\"} — revision string: \"0\"? The example says revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Addition 0\" is the revision label. I'll use \"0\"? Hmm: \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)\". The table says \"Addition 0\". I'll put \"Addition 0\". Or revision \"0\" with changes \"Addition 0: Issuing certificate\"? I'll go revision: \"0\", changes: \"Issuing certificate (Addition 0)\". Hmm, simpler: revision \"Addition 0\". I'll use \"0\" since the instruction hints revision should be like \"0\". Let me do revision: \"0\", date: \"2021-10-15\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\". And maybe note addition. Fine.\n\nfootnotes: none in document. Omit.\n\nNow date formats: use ISO \"2021-10-15\".\n\nMPE lower: ±5% → value {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\". Label: \"maximum_permitted_error_lower_zone\"? I'll use \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\" and \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\".\n\nMPE upper config:\n- {attribute: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", axis: \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", value: {min:-2, max:2}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"water temperature > 30 °C\", value: {min:-3, max:3}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]}\n\nWait — structured form for config values: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\" per rule 5. But rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For config_level, rule 5 shows {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll include unit_symbol at that level (and value plain). Hmm, inconsistent schema... I'll follow rule 5's shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Maybe also include unit_id null? I'll keep {condition, value:{min,max}, unit_symbol:\"%\"}.\n\nActually, to be consistent with rule 2's strict shape, maybe config values should also be full objects: {condition: ..., value: ..., unit_symbol: ...}. Rule 5 explicitly: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" OK good.\n\nTemperature class value: \"T30, T50\" — as string? Or could be config axis? It's a set of permissible classes: string \"T30 / T50\". I'll use string \"T30, T50\". Hmm the rule about lists-of-values being model matrix — but T30/T50 are class designations, both apply to all models. String fine.\n\nWater pressure class: \"MAP 10, MAP 16\" string. MAP = maximum admissible pressure in bar (MAP10 = 10 bar = 1 MPa; MAP16 = 1.6 MPa, consistent with max admissible pressure 1.6 MPa).\n\nEnvironmental class: \"B or O\" string.\nElectromagnetic environment: \"E1, E2\" string.\nInstallation sensitivity class: \"U0D0\".\n\nIndicating range: 999999 m³. Resolution indicating device: 0.001 m³. Rapid testing resolution: 0.00001 m³.\n\nMax admissible temperature: 50 °C. Max admissible pressure: 1.6 MPa.\n\nPower: type \"non-replaceable battery\"; umax 3.6 V; umin 1.9 V; battery life 10 years. Frequency: \"-\" omit.\n\nSoftware versions: type_level characteristic \"legally_relevant_software_version_crc\": \"03.00 / 5563; 04.01 / 235E; 04.10 / 62C7611F\" — string value.\n\nAlso software display format Axx.xx etc. — description.\n\nRadio module frequencies 433/434/868 MHz: \"Legally non-relevant part of communication with meter is possible by radio module with frequency 433 MHz or 434 MHz or 868 MHz\" — optional, not part of certificate (\"can be equipped by radio module which is not part of this certificate\"). I'll mention in description only.\n\nHigh resolution mode resolution: 0.00001 m³ (matches rapid testing resolution row).\n\nNow unit_ids: I'll leave null mostly; could set \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C per example. Rule says leave null unless certain. The example in rule 3 shows unit_id \"u:degree_Celsius\" as a valid ID. I'm fairly confident about u:degree_Celsius since it's given in the prompt. But to be safe, I'll set unit_id null everywhere except maybe °C. The instruction: \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" The only ID given in the prompt is u:degree_Celsius. I'll use it for °C fields and null elsewhere. Hmm, m³/h etc. unknown → null.\n\nStructured value objects: each value must include unit_symbol and unit_id? Rule 2 shape: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. footnote_markers optional ([] when none? The shape shows it with footnote_markers; rule 7 uses footnote_markers when referenced). I'll include footnote_markers: [] only where needed... To be safe, include \"footnote_markers\": [] everywhere? That's verbose. The shape shows it but rules only require markers when referenced. I'll include empty arrays for consistency in type_level but maybe omit in model_level for brevity. Hmm — consistency is better for a parser. Let me include footnote_markers: [] everywhere. That's a lot of repetition but safe.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: including \"footnote_markers\": [] in every value object makes the JSON huge but valid. I'll do it. Hmm, ~200 value objects × small array... fine.\n\nActually wait, for dimensionless values like accuracy_class 2, unit_symbol null, unit_id null.\n\nLet me now also decide: q2_over_q1 = 1.6, q3_over_q4 = 1.25 at type_level (dimensionless numbers).\n\nLabel naming (canonical snake_case):\n- \"q1\" → \"q1\" or \"minimum_flowrate_q1\"? I'll use \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\" (standard water meter notation), plus \"q3_over_q1\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q3_over_q4\".\n- nominal_diameter → \"nominal_diameter_dn\" with unit null. I'll use \"nominal_diameter_dn\".\n- pressure_loss_class → \"pressure_loss_class\".\n- maximum_admissible_temperature → \"maximum_admissible_temperature\".\n- maximum_admissible_pressure → \"maximum_admissible_pressure\".\n- indicating_range → \"indicating_range\".\n- resolution_of_indicating_device → \"indicating_device_resolution\".\n- resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing → \"rapid_test_device_resolution\".\n- installation_sensitivity_class → \"installation_sensitivity_class\".\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe → \"minimum_straight_length_inlet_pipe\".\n- outlet → \"minimum_straight_length_outlet_pipe\".\n- flow_conditioner → \"flow_conditioner_required\": value \"No\" (string) — or boolean false. Value must be number|range|string → \"No\".\n- power_supply_type → \"power_supply_type\": \"non-replaceable battery\".\n- umax → \"u_max\", unit V. umin → \"u_min\".\n- battery life → \"minimum_battery_life_time\", unit \"years\".\n- eut category/case → \"eut_test_category\", \"eut_test_case\".\n- accuracy_class → \"accuracy_class\": 2.\n- measuring_principle → \"measuring_principle\": \"ultrasonic\".\n- temperature_class → \"temperature_class\": \"T30, T50\".\n- water_pressure_class → \"water_pressure_class\": \"MAP 10, MAP 16\".\n- environmental_class → \"environmental_class\": \"B or O\".\n- electromagnetic_environment → \"electromagnetic_environment_class\": \"E1, E2\".\n- orientation → \"orientation_limitation\": \"any\".\n- software → \"software_version_crc_legally_relevant\": \"03.00 / 5563; 04.01 / 235E; 04.10 / 62C7611F\".\n- mpe labels as above.\n\nFor model_level attribute names: \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"connection_type_screw_thread\", \"length_brass_body\", \"length_composite_body\".\n\nUnits: q3/q4 in m³/h; lengths mm.\n\nNow, unit_symbol \"m³/h\" — keep unicode ³. Yes.\n\nNow descriptions:\ncertified_type.description: \"Ultrasonic water meter for measuring, memorising and displaying the volume of water at metering conditions. Brass or composite body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and an electronic indicating device with LCD. Normal and high resolution modes (0.00001 m³ in high resolution mode); adjustment and reading/setting of metrological data via NFC, protected by password. Optional radio module (433/434/868 MHz) is legally non-relevant and not part of the certificate. Separated software; SW versions and CRCs shown on LCD. Installed to operate in arbitrary orientation; not designed to measure reverse flow.\"\n\nNow matrix_tables with column_units arrays per the R137 example (columns + column_units). \n\nFlowrates table 1: name \"Flowrates — UL 2.5 and UL 4\". columns: [\"model_number\", \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"q3_over_q1\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\"], column_units: [null, null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"]. Rows as objects. Numeric values.\n\nOrder in the doc: q1, q2, q3, q4 rows; columns are ratio. I'll restructure rows per ratio.\n\nRows T1:\n{\"model_number\": \"UL 2.5\", \"nominal_diameter_dn\": \"15 / 20\", \"q3_over_q1\": 800, \"q1\": 0.003, \"q2\": 0.005, \"q3\": 2.50, \"q4\": 3.13}\n{...400: q1 0.006, q2 0.010}\n{...250: q1 0.010, q2 0.016}\n{\"UL 4\", 20, 800, 0.005, 0.008, 4.00, 5.00}\n{400: 0.010, 0.016}\n{250: 0.016, 0.026}\n\nT2 (UL 6.3 DN25; UL 10 DN25/32):\n{\"UL 6.3\", 25, 800, 0.008, 0.013, 6.30, 7.88}\n{400, 0.016, 0.025}\n{250, 0.025, 0.040}\n{\"UL 10\", \"25 / 32\", 800, 0.013, 0.020, 10.00, 12.50}\n{400, 0.025, 0.040}\n{250, 0.040, 0.064}\n\nT3 (UL 16, DN 40): 5 rows:\n{Q3=10, R400: q1 0.025, q2 0.040, q4 12.50}\n{Q3=10, R250: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 12.50}\n{Q3=16, R800: q1 0.020, q2 0.032, q4 20.00}\n{Q3=16, R400: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 20.00}\n{Q3=16, R250: q1 0.064, q2 0.102, q4 20.00}\nColumns: model_number, nominal_diameter_dn, q3, q3_over_q1, q1, q2, q4.\n\nT4 (UL 25, DN 50):\n{500: q1 0.050, q2 0.080, q3 25.0, q4 31.3}\n{400: 0.0625